so it's Thursday meaning we once again  have new unemployment numbers from last  week the numbers are not good 2.4  million more people filed for  unemployment benefits last week and this  in total now is starting to be  staggering just unconscionable numbers  when we total up the job losses from  these 9 or so weeks of coronavirus  unemployment pandemic area era maybe  it's 10 weeks at this point something  like that we are now at 38 point six  million jobs lost if on February 1st  anybody had said my prediction is by  June 1st we will have lost 40 million  jobs which is what it will probably be  after we add another week or two of  numbers I don't think anybody would have  believed it now if you want to find some  kind of like it could be worse type of  thing in the numbers what we can point  to is at least the numbers are declining  each week in terms of new people losing  their jobs in early April each week we  had like more than 6 million people  filed for new unemployment benefits and  then by the end of April early May we  were at 4 million a week 3 million a  week and it sounds crazy but that number  is now down to only 2.4 million more  jobs lost last week so in a sense we're  seeing a decreased rate of people losing  their jobs but once again even with  these diminishing numbers by mid-july we  could be at 50 million 50 million people  having lost their jobs now let's do a  little bit of context because this is  important when we talk about this the  total workforce before the pandemic hit  was estimated at 158 million people so  the country is 331 million people many  of those are children many of those are  retired people so the the the active  working labor force was believed to be a  hundred and fifty-eight million people  so thirty-eight point six million losing  their jobs means that 24.4 call it 25  percent about a quarter of the American  workforce lost jobs during the corona  virus pandemic now we've got to adjust  those numbers a little bit one way a  little bit the other way one reality is  these are probably under estimates  because there are lots of people who  lost jobs but for one reason or another  didn't file unemployment benefits there  may be people who lost a job but they're  independently wealthy or have another  source of income or whatever and they  just decided I'm not gonna go through  the hassle of filing unemployment that's  a small group of people you have a  larger group of people who would file an  unemployment claim except it's a what we  might call a marginal job or a job that  is under the table in which case you  wouldn't be able to file for  unemployment benefits and there is still  some fraction of people who have just  been struggling to get the claim in  because systems have been overloaded  unemployment is run at the state level  50 states 50 different systems some are  mostly phones some are online  combination of the two so in a sense  these numbers are probably too low now  on the other side of this there are  people who lost jobs early on but have  found new jobs so we have a cumulative  total of 38 point six million  unemployment claims in the last nine ten  eleven weeks but some of those people  who lost their job in March for example  have found a new job we don't know  exactly what number that is  but there are businesses that have been  hiring delivery business based  businesses grocery stores places like  Amazon and others have been hiring so  some of the people that lost jobs have  found new jobs now what we can be sure  of is given this bad news the stock  market is gonna do really really well  today and I'm sort of kidding but we've  seen a stock market that's increasingly  divorced from the unemployment news now  as we've talked about before  stock markets often react to how the  news compares to expectations so these  new numbers of about two point forty  four million more people losing their  jobs last week it's more or less in line  with expectation so it may not move the  market very very much so what we may  actually see really shake up the stock  market would be the numbers that we're  going to get about the second quarter in  total so q2  earnings and q2 GDP may be numbers that  really move the stock market potentially  down but maybe not so first we've got  earnings seasons how much did companies  make during the second quarter and  typically we get those numbers for the  second quarter which is April May June  around July 15th we'll start to get  earnings numbers from companies and that  may push the market up it may pull the  market down then we will get GDP numbers  even later on so we'll get initial GDP  estimates for the second quarter on July  30th and then we'll get more accurate  second quarter GDP estimates towards the  end of August so realize that we are not  going to know the full scope of what  happened in this quarter right now  essentially until mid to late August  almost September in practice there are  predictions that we could see a 20% GDP  decline in q2 25% one estimate said 35  percent that's certainly on the very  high end so this is about suffering it's  not about politics but we can't ignore  that historically bad GDP numbers the  worst GDP numbers the country has ever  seen  eight weeks before a presidential  election we will know the full scope of  those those numbers again late August  that may be very very bad for Donald  Trump now for now as states have been  improving on their coronavirus numbers  and putting in place plans to get some  portions of their state economies back  to work my hope is because again for me  this is not political  I'd want things to improve economically  and medically as quickly as is  reasonably possible some portions of  people are going to be getting back to  work hopefully things will tick up a bit  or at least least continue taking down  less and reminder that the most  important takeaway here if we want to  take what's going on and turn it into  action for the long term we've got to  fix the problem now of course over the  long term the lesson here is even  economies that look really good by all  of the traditional indicators are really  really fragile our lack of preparedness  has been exposed here there will be more  pandemics there are going to be more  downturns we can't control that what we  can control is the underlying fragility  of our economic system and it's been  exposed as very very fragile
 

